      "content": "The Electoral College significantly shapes American elections by prioritizing states over national populations, often favoring \"swing states\" where a candidate must focus their political strategy. This system can result in a winner by popular vote losing the presidency, a possibility that no other proposed voting reform allows. Consequently, smaller states maintain disproportionately high political influence compared to their population size, which some argue strengthens state rights while others claim it undermines the principle of \"one person, one vote.\" Ultimately, the Electoral College continues to complicate the democratic mandate because the electors chosen by each state do not always vote in direct proportion to how the public voted at the national level.",
